875, -0.01129150390625, -0.01155853271484375, -0.0201568603515625, 0.0333251953125, -0.034820556640625, 0.00537109375, 0.039703369140625, 0.0250701904296875, -0.006587982177734375, 0.006740570068359375, 0.006557464599609375, 0.012969970703125, 0.021392822265625, -0.0126190185546875, -0.036651611328125, 0.0240631103515625, 0.0182342529296875, -0.00983428955078125, -0.0173492431640625, 0.0064239501953125, -0.0277557373046875, -0.01132965087890625, 0.0214385986328125, -0.0041046142578125, -0.005031585693359375, 0.022308349609375, -0.02825927734375, 0.01090240478515625, -0.0211944580078125, 0.004283905029296875, -0.007297515869140625, 0.0254974365234375, 0.03228759765625, 0.0299224853515625, -0.004611968994140625, 0.017181396484375, 0.0389404296875, -0.012176513671875, 0.0159759521484375, -0.0166015625, 0.0206756591796875, 0.03790283203125, -0.0258941650390625, 0.0228271484375, 0.0004324913024902344, 0.0193328857421875, 0.0232086181640625, -0.0158843994140625, 0.01142120361328125, 0.0158233642578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction**\n\nDistrict heating networks are a cornerstone of urban decarbonization, enabling the integration of renewable energy sources and large-scale thermal storage. However, their efficient operation is fundamentally challenged by the nonlinear physics of heat transport, the presence of cycles in meshed topologies, and the need to coordinate multiple suppliers and storage units. These challenges are exacerbated by the increasing complexity of modern networks, which often feature time-varying renewable inputs and require accurate modeling of temperature-dependent heat losses.\n\n**Research Gap and Motivation**\n\nDespite significant progress, there remains a need for optimization frameworks that can simultaneously address nonlinear heat losses, meshed (cyclic) topologies, multiple suppliers, and storage units in a unified and globally optimal manner. Most existing approaches are limited to tree-like networks, rely on linearized or convexified models, or provide heuristic solutions without guarantees of global optimality. This work aims to address these limitations by formulating and solving the following stationary district heating network optimization problem:\n\n**Formal Problem Statement:**  \nGiven a directed graph \\( G = (V, E) \\) representing the district heating network, with sets of suppliers \\( S \\), consumers \\( D \\), storage nodes \\( V_{stor} \\), and mixing nodes \\( M \\), determine the mass flows \\( f_{ik} \\), inlet and outlet temperatures \\( T_{ik}^{\\text{in}}, T_{ik}^{\\text{out}} \\), supplier heat injections \\( x_i \\), and storage states \\( s_j \\) that minimize the total supplier cost \\( \\sum_{i \\in S} C_i(x_i) \\), subject to:\n- Nonlinear heat loss equations along each pipe,\n- Flow and temperature coupling at mixing nodes,\n- Mass and energy balance at all nodes,\n- Supplier, demand, and storage constraints,\n- Physical bounds on flows and temperatures,\n- Explicit cycle constraints to ensure physical feasibility in meshed networks.\n\n**2. Related Work**\n\nEarly approaches to district heating optimization focused on mixed-integer linear programming (MILP) [1], which linearizes network physics and is tractable only for acyclic (tree-like) networks. These methods cannot capture nonlinear heat losses or flow-temperature coupling, and the number of binary variables grows exponentially with network cycles, making them unsuitable for meshed topologies.\n\nConvex relaxation techniques [2] have been developed to approximate nonlinearities, such as piecewise-linearization of heat loss, but these are typically limited to small or weakly meshed networks and rely on simplifying assumptions that break down for realistic, cyclic topologies. The relaxations may not be tight, leading to suboptimal or infeasible solutions.\n\nHeuristic methods [3] can handle larger and more complex networks, including meshed topologies and multiple suppliers or storage units, but they do not provide guarantees of global optimality. Their performance is highly problem-dependent, and they may fail to find feasible or high-quality solutions in challenging cases.\n\nRecent advances (e.g., [8,9], and post-2020 works such as [11,12,13,14]) have explored improved heuristics, decomposition methods, and tighter convex relaxations for small cycles or specific network structures. For example, [11] introduces a decomposition-based approach for cyclic networks but does not address nonlinear heat losses, while [12] considers storage but only in tree-like topologies. [13] and [14] propose scalable heuristics for large networks but lack global optimality guarantees and do not model all nonlinearities. To our knowledge, no existing work provides a global optimization framework that simultaneously addresses nonlinear heat losses, general meshed topologies, multiple suppliers, and storage units without significant simplifications.\n\n**3. Summary of Methodological Features**\n\nTable 1 summarizes the capabilities of representative methods. For clarity, the features are defined as follows:  \n- *Meshed networks*: Support for general network topologies with cycles.  \n- *Nonlinear heat loss*: Accurate modeling of temperature-dependent heat losses along pipes.  \n- *Multiple suppliers/storage*: Ability to model networks with more than one heat supplier and with thermal storage units.  \n- *Global optimality guarantees*: The method can certify that a solution is globally optimal for the original (nonlinear, nonconvex) problem.  \n- *Explicit cycle elimination*: The algorithm includes constraints or procedures to ensure that flow variables respect the physical laws around cycles (e.g., Kirchhoff-type constraints), preventing unphysical solutions.  \n- *Symmetry exploitation*: The method identifies and removes redundant solutions arising from network symmetries, reducing computational effort.  \n- *Constraint reduction*: Techniques to eliminate redundant or inactive constraints, improving scalability.  \n- *Primal heuristics*: Algorithms that quickly generate feasible solutions (not necessarily optimal) to provide upper bounds or warm starts for global optimization.\n\n**Table 1: Comparison of Optimization Methods for District Heating Networks**\n\n| Feature                        | [1] MILP | [2] Convex Relaxation | [3] Heuristic | This Work [*] |\n|--------------------------------|----------|----------------------|---------------|---------------|\n| Meshed networks                | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713             |\n| Nonlinear heat loss            | \u2717        | \u2713 (approximate)      | \u2713             | \u2713             |\n| Multiple suppliers/storage     | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713             |\n| Global optimality guarantees   | \u2713        | \u2713 (relaxed)          | \u2717             | \u2713             |\n| Explicit cycle elimination     | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713             |\n| Symmetry exploitation          | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713             |\n| Constraint reduction           | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2717             | \u2713             |\n| Primal heuristics              | \u2717        | \u2717                    | \u2713             | \u2713             |\n\n*Legend: \"\u2713\" = full support; \"\u2713 (approximate)\" = partial or approximate support (e.g., via linearization); \"\u2717\" = not supported. [*] This Work refers to the method proposed in this paper.*\n\n**Additional Notes:**  \n- Scalability and computational complexity are not directly comparable across methods due to differences in problem formulations and implementation details. In practice, MILP and convex relaxations scale poorly with network cycles, while heuristics are more scalable but may yield suboptimal solutions.  \n- Solution quality for heuristics and relaxations is problem-dependent and may not be globally optimal.  \n- See [11,12,13,14] for recent benchmarks and scalability studies.\n\n**4. Contributions**\n\nThis work makes the following key contributions:\n- **Unified Global Optimization Framework:** We propose a global optimization approach for stationary, nonlinear district heating networks with general (meshed) topologies, multiple suppliers, and thermal storage, without relying on linearization or convex relaxation.\n- **Accurate Physical Modeling:** Our method models nonlinear heat losses and flow-temperature coupling exactly, ensuring physical fidelity.\n- **Explicit Cycle Constraints:** We introduce explicit cycle constraints to enforce physical feasibility in meshed networks, preventing unphysical flow solutions.\n- **Symmetry and Constraint Reduction:** The algorithm exploits network symmetries and eliminates redundant constraints, significantly improving computational efficiency.\n- **Global Optimality and Heuristics:** We provide global optimality guarantees and incorporate primal heuristics for rapid feasible solution generation.\n- **Algorithmic Overview:** The approach combines a branch-and-bound global optimization framework with custom cycle constraint generation, symmetry detection, and primal heuristics for warm-starting. Cycle constraints are constructed using Paton's algorithm [10], and redundant constraints are identified via graph-theoretic analysis.\n\n**5. Notation and Definitions**\n\nA comprehensive notation table is provided below for clarity. Units and domains are specified where applicable.\n\n| Symbol                        | Description                                                                                 | Units / Domain                |\n|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| \\( V \\)                       | Set of all nodes (junctions, consumers, suppliers, storage units, mixing nodes)            | Set                           |\n| \\( E \\subseteq V \\times V \\)  | Set of directed edges (pipes) in the network graph \\( G = (V, E) \\)                        | Set                           |\n| \\( S \\subseteq V \\)           | Set of supplier nodes (heat injection)                                                     | Set                           |\n| \\( D \\subseteq V \\)           | Set of demand (consumer) nodes (heat withdrawal)                                           | Set                           |\n| \\( M \\subseteq V \\)           | Set of mixing nodes (nodes with multiple incoming edges; perfect mixing assumed)           | Set                           |\n| \\( V_{stor} \\subseteq V \\)    | Set of storage nodes (nodes with thermal storage)                                          | Set                           |\n| \\( \\mathcal{C} \\)             | Set of fundamental cycles in \\( G \\), constructed via Paton's algorithm [10]               | Set                           |\n| \\( d_i \\)                     | Net demand at node \\( i \\) (\\( >0 \\) for consumers, \\( <0 \\) for suppliers, \\( =0 \\) else)| MW (megawatts)                |\n| \\( C_i(x_i) \\)                | Cost function for supplier \\( i \\)                                                         | Currency / time               |\n| \\( \\alpha_{ik} \\)             | Heat loss coefficient for pipe \\( (i,k) \\)                                                 | MW/K/m                        |\n| \\( L_{ik} \\)                  | Length of pipe \\( (i,k) \\)                                                                | m (meters)                    |\n| \\( c_p \\)                     | Specific heat capacity of water                                                            | kJ/(kg\u00b7K)                     |\n| \\( T_{amb} \\)                 | Ambient temperature                                                                       | \u00b0C or K                       |\n| \\( T_{\\min}, T_{\\max} \\)      | Minimum and maximum allowable temperatures                                                 | \u00b0C or K                       |\n| \\( f_{ik} \\geq 0 \\)           | Mass flow on edge \\("}}